Other bundled material
----------------------
- Colour palettes (eleven, in three groups; docs/palettes.md in the source repository):
  (1) Seven colour modes reproduced from depthmapX v0.9.1 (salalib/pafcolor.cpp and salalib/displayparams.h, tag v0.9.1;
  GPL-3.0-or-later, Copyright (C) 2011-2012 Tasos Varoudis): the colour values, and the depthmapX Classic mapping, which was
  reimplemented in R to reproduce depthmapX behaviour. The maintainer has decided to retain the package licence LGPL (>= 3) and this
  attribution; that is a maintainer decision, not a legal opinion or a formally verified clearance (docs/licensing.md, open item 6:
  whether the reproduction of depthmapX colour values and behaviour in an LGPL-3-or-later package needs further review).
  (2) Two white-background adaptations (Axmanesque White, depthmapX Classic White): rPSTool adaptations that reuse some depthmapX
  colours (seven Axmanesque White anchors are entries of the depthmapX Equal Ranges (3-Colour) array; three depthmapX Classic White
  anchors equal the Classic cyan, green and red landmark colours); the other anchors are not depthmapX values. The depthmapX
  attribution above applies to the reused colours.
  (3) Two palettes designed for rPSTool (Coral Night, Coral Day).
  The depthmapX Blue-Red and Purple-Orange values coincide with the 7-class ColorBrewer RdYlBu and PuOr schemes (Brewer et al.);
  ColorBrewer's licence terms were not verified (docs/licensing.md, open item 9).
- Example data (inst/extdata/hillier_iida/): the Barnsbury example data are derived from Hillier, B. and Iida, S. (2005), Network and
  Psychological Effects in Urban Movement, LNCS 3693, pp. 475-490 (DOI 10.1007/11556114_30), and include the axial network, unlinks and
  observed movement gate counts; the original data were obtained from https://discovery.ucl.ac.uk/id/eprint/1232/. The geometries
  were georeferenced to EPSG:27700 and spatially transformed (best-fit transformation) by Kimon Krenz; the coordinates are therefore
  not the original, unmodified coordinates. The data are included and redistributed with permission from the authors. The original
  research and data remain attributable to their respective authors. The movement gate counts were originally reported by Penn, A.,
  Hillier, B., Banister, D. and Xu, J. (1998), Configurational modelling of urban movement networks, Environment and Planning B:
  Planning and Design, 25(1), pp. 59-84 (DOI 10.1068/b250059). The package licence (LGPL-3-or-later) applies to rPSTool's software; the
  Barnsbury data are included with the authors' permission and remain attributable to them (docs/example-data.md).
